+## How verified
+
+**Link check — method and result.** `python3 scripts/check_docs.py`, run through `bench-verify`:
+**127 references checked, 0 dangling; 58 files under `docs/`, 27 index entries, 0 problems.** The
+checker enforces two properties: (a) every markdown link in every tracked `.md`, and every
+`docs/…` path written anywhere in the tree (README, shell scripts, module docstrings), resolves
+to a file that exists; (b) every file under `docs/` is named exactly once by an index entry — a
+bullet opening with its path in backticks — or by a directory entry covering it. Fenced code
+blocks are skipped (a `PAYLOADS[event_type](**payload)` line parses as a markdown link
+otherwise), and pinned `path@sha` refs are excluded, since those name a path at a commit, not at
+HEAD. Deliberately outside the enforced set: shorthand module names (`board/legality.py`),
+container paths, and paths into other repos — enforcing those buys noise, not integrity. They are
+reported by `--advisory`, which also lists the 62 places a doc is named without a path; all of
+them are unambiguous, and the INDEX is the redirect.
+
+**Manual verification of the triage itself.** Each verdict was read against the document before
+acting: the `hive_user_migration` verdict was contradicted by its own revision record (above); the
+other thirteen held. Status claims in the INDEX were checked against the tree, not inferred from
+the docs (see item 6).
+
+**Review.** One bounded `/code-review` pass at low effort against the code clone — matched to a
+diff that is moves, headers and one 164-line script. One finding, accepted and fixed in
+`5d0d733`: `check_docs.py` read `git ls-files` split on whitespace, which would shred any tracked
+path containing a space; it now reads NUL-separated. Latent today (no such path exists), real on
+the first one. No other findings.
+
+**Not run: the repo test suite.** `pytest` needs live Postgres and this task's environment has
+none; `uv sync` needs the network. Nothing in the diff touches importable code — the only
+non-documentation change is a new standalone script, and the two `ladder/` edits are a README line
+and a docstring path. `ruff check scripts/check_docs.py` passes against the repo's config; `mypy`
+does not cover `scripts/`.
+
+## To operate
+
+- **The branch is `docs-triage` in the code clone, unmerged.** Review and merge it as the usual
+  PR; nothing in it needs a deploy, a restart, or a migration.
+- **Re-run the check any time:** `python3 scripts/check_docs.py` from the repo root (add
+  `--advisory` for the name-mention list). Exit 0 means zero dangling references and a complete,
+  non-duplicating index. **It is not wired into CI** — one step in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`
+  beside ruff would make link integrity standing rather than per-pass, but adding a CI gate was
+  outside a moves-and-labels order, so it is yours to decide.
+- **Two decisions are yours, both cheap:**
+  1. `omegahive_hive_user_migration.md` stayed in `docs/`. If Part D has run since the survey,
+     say so and it moves to `docs/archive/` with a one-line header correction.
+  2. The eight unsurveyed work orders stayed in `docs/` with honest Status lines. The five
+     executed ones are archive candidates on the same convention that archived `stage2_spec`; the
+     two open ones are not, and `c2_battery_spec` is current. That is a survey verdict, not
+     mine to invent.
+- **Coordination with readme-restructure:** this touched exactly two places in `README.md` — the
+  fenced repo-map block and the paragraph immediately after it — plus three inline doc links
+  (lines 5, 35, 280) whose targets moved. Whichever lands second reconciles those; the INDEX is
+  the redirect for any pointer that goes stale.
+- **The result announcement is not emitted.** This instrument's scope forbids spine emits, so the
+  `task.result_posted` for this report has to come from elsewhere.
+
+## Reflection
+
+**The order's own numbers were the most useful input and the least reliable one.** "~14 top-level
+`omegahive_*.md` files" is what the survey saw; the directory holds 22. The eight it missed are a
+coherent class — self-contained work orders and post-audit fix lists — which is probably why they
+fell out of a survey framed around "three eras" of specs. They are exactly the docs an outsider
+most misreads, because a file called `omegahive_v0b_order.md` looks identical whether it shipped
+last month or is still waiting on a gate. The cheap fix (one Status line each, verified against
+the code) is in; the disposition question is left open rather than answered by a worker who did
+not run the survey.
+
+**The named seam fired, and the doc's own revision record is what caught it.** The triage called
+`hive_user_migration` an executed procedure; its last paragraph says Part D is deliberately
+unexecuted. Reading the *end* of a long document is what distinguished a closed procedure from a
+half-run one — the head of the file reads entirely like a finished migration. Worth generalizing:
+a survey verdict of "executed" should be checked against the doc's revision record and its
+callers, not its opening.
+
+**The spend the prediction did not name was validation, not moves.** The order priced this as
+"mechanical moves, one index, link verification," with link-chasing as the thing keeping it off
+the median floor. The moves were six `git mv`s. What actually consumed the work was checking each
+verdict against its document and each status claim against the code — is the UI really shipped, is
+D1 really in `gateway.py`, does a `v0a-r2` record exist — because the INDEX's whole value is that
+its status column is true. Link-chasing was cheap once written as a script; the script also found
+14 dangling links the order did not know about, all of them created by the *previous* archive
+pass, which is the strongest argument for wiring the check into CI.
+
+**A deliberate scope call.** The order says moves and labels only. A committed checker is neither,
+but the definition of done asks for a recorded link-check method, and "I ran a grep" is not a
+method the next docs pass can re-run. One script with no dependencies seemed a better answer than
+a paragraph of instructions; the CI step that would make it binding is left as a proposal above,
+not taken.
+
+**Effort:** first to last commit on the branch spans **18:12 to 18:21** (`git log` committer
+timestamps, `823f019..5d0d733`), which measures the commit window, not the reading that preceded
+it — the survey validation above happened before the first commit and is not in that span. Six
+commits at real boundaries: moves, labels+links, INDEX+checker, README, the unsurveyed eight, the
+review fix.
